Market Challenges
Technical Limitations and Sample Interference
Raman spectroscopy can face technical limitations when samples produce weak signals or contain substances that cause fluorescence interference. Such interference can obscure useful spectral information and complicate analysis, particularly when examining certain biological samples and organic compounds.

Addressing these limitations may require specialized lasers, advanced detection techniques, or additional analytical steps, which can increase the complexity of testing.

Sensitivity and Detection Limits
Detecting trace-level compounds remains challenging for some conventional Raman systems. Enhanced techniques can improve sensitivity, but they may require specialized substrates, careful sample handling, or additional preparation. These requirements can affect the practicality of routine testing in certain environments.

Standardization and Reproducibility
Differences in instrument calibration, laser settings, operating conditions, and data-processing methods can influence results. Maintaining consistency across instruments and laboratories is important for dependable analysis, especially when measurements are used for quality control, research comparisons, or regulated applications.

